      "message": " enabled and the config name is a fixed identifier\" \u2014 is copied into the file as literal constants rather than imported.\n\n## Local check\n\nRan a small in-process check asserting `CONFIG_NAME` equals `\"E_unrelated_config_v1\"`, that `feature_flag()` is `True` and identical across 100 repeated calls, and that `__all__` lists exactly the two public names. It passed cleanly (exit 0, no assertion failures).\n\nTwo notes on the process:\n- My first `run_command` was rejected because it began with `cd` (only Python commands are permitted); I re-ran it as pure Python.\n- The tool returned empty stdout on each successful run, so I made the final check fail loudly via `assert`/`SystemExit` to confirm correctness through the exit code alone rather than printed output.\n\n**Constraints respected:** I did not read `semkg_contract` and did not create any file other than the requested one. The `__pycache__/` directory created by Python's import machinery is a byproduct of the check, not an authored artifact."
